ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE PORT AUTHORITY ACT

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Port Authority Act and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 1-2 (Establishment of port authorities)
① Port authorities (hereinafter referred to as "Authorities") shall be established at Busan Port, Incheon Port and Ulsan Port, respectively, under the main sentence of Article 4 (2) of the Port Authority Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Act"). <Amended on 2011.8.11, 2017.6.20>
② Under the proviso to Article 4 (2) of the Act, a port authority having jurisdiction over Yeosu Port and Gwangyang Port shall be established. <Newly Inserted on 2011.8.11; Amended on 2017.6.20>

Article 2 (Jurisdiction of Authorities)
① "Port areas of ports prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main sentence of Article 4 (4) of the Act means the port areas listed in the attached Table. <Newly Inserted on 2017.6.20>
② "Port facilities, etc. outside port areas prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso to Article 4 (4) of the Act means the following facilities outside port areas designated and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries for each Authority: <Amended on 2013.3.23, 2017.6.20>
1. Water facilities and mooring facilities among basic facilities under Article 2, subparagraph 5 (a) of the Harbor Act;
2. Functional facilities under Article 2, subparagraph 5 (b) of the Harbor Act;
3. Support facilities under Article 2, subparagraph 5 (c) of the Harbor Act.

Article 3 (Matters to be stated in articles of incorporation)
To ensure transparency and accountability in management, the articles of incorporation of an Authority shall include:
1. Establishment of management objectives;
2. Reporting management performance, etc.;
3. Evaluation of management performance;
4. Preparation of budgets and settlement of accounts;
5. Establishment of operational plans;
6. Public disclosure of management.

Article 4 (Business)
① "Port facilities prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as outer harbor facilities and port transportation facilities" in Article 8 (1) 1 of the Act means any of the following port facilities under Article 2, subparagraph 5 of the Harbor Act: <Amended on 2011.8.11, 2021.1.5>
1. Outer harbor facilities, including breakwaters, sand barriers, wave barriers, seawalls, training dikes, locks and revetments (facilities preventing erosion of banks and shores);
2. Port transportation facilities, including roads, bridges, railways, tracks and canals;
3. Navigational aids, including facilities related to navigation marks, signals, lighting and port communications for the arrival and departure of vessels;
4. Port control facilities;
5. Deleted. <2012.8.28>
6. Port facilities developed by the State to facilitate prompt construction of new ports under Article 2, subparagraph 1 of the New Harbor Construction Promotion Act (including water facilities but excluding port facilities developed by an Authority under Article 21-2 of the Act).
② "Projects prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 8 (1) 7 of the Act means:
1. Construction of foreign ports;
2. Management and operation of foreign ports;
3. Establishment and operation of infrastructure relating to port logistics information.
③ When the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries establishes and implements an infrastructure project master plan under Article 10 of the Act on Public-Private Partnerships in Infrastructure and a master plan under Article 3 of the New Harbor Construction Promotion Act concerning port facilities pursued as public-private partnership projects under the former Act or construction projects for port facilities under paragraph ① 6, the Minister shall hear the opinion of the Authority. <Amended on 2012.8.28, 2013.3.23>
④ For security, safety and cargo management, etc. at ports under Article 8 (1) 1 of the Act, an Authority may request the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries to provide vessel location information obtained from ship position transmitting devices under Article 30 of the Ship Safety Act. <Amended on 2013.3.23>

Article 5 (Composition of port committees)
① The port committee of an Authority (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee") shall consist of seven non-standing members.
② Under Article 11 (2) of the Act, the Metropolitan Mayor, Do Governor or Special Self-Governing Province Governor having jurisdiction over the location of the port shall recommend up to three persons (including one representative of organizations of users of the port) as non-standing members. <Amended on 2017.6.20>

Article 6 (Qualifications of committee members)
Committee members shall be persons falling under any of the following:
1. Associate professors or higher at universities with extensive knowledge and experience in shipping, ports and logistics;
2. Senior researchers or higher at research institutes funded by the Government or local governments with extensive knowledge and experience in shipping, ports and logistics;
3. Certified public accountants, attorneys or experts in management consultancy with at least five years of practical experience;
4. Public officials of Grade IV or higher or general-service public officials in the Senior Civil Service Corps whose duties relate to those of an Authority, including shipping, ports and logistics: Provided, That this shall not apply to a committee of an Authority designated as a public enterprise under Article 5 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
5. Persons with at least three years' experience as executives of organizations of port users or enterprises related to shipping, ports and logistics;
6. Other persons recognized as having knowledge and experience in business management, logistics or related fields.

Article 6-2 (Composition and operation of committees for recommending executive officers)
① Where it becomes necessary to appoint an executive officer due to expiration of a term or for any other reason, the Committee shall establish without delay a committee for recommending executive officers under Article 16-2 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Recommendation Committee").
② The number of members of the Recommendation Committee shall be determined by resolution of the Committee within a range of five to 15: Provided, That where three or fewer members are serving on the Committee at the time of establishment, the Recommendation Committee may consist of three or four members.
③ Members selected by the Committee under Article 16-2 (2) of the Act shall be selected from among persons with extensive knowledge and experience in various fields, including law, business, the press, academia and labor: Provided, That one person capable of representing the opinions of members of the Authority shall be included.
④ The Recommendation Committee shall adopt resolutions with the affirmative vote of a majority of its incumbent members.
⑤ The Recommendation Committee may entrust the solicitation and investigation of executive officer candidates, etc. to specialized institutions.
⑥ Matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Recommendation Committee, including exclusion, challenge and recusal of members, and selection of executive officers, other than those prescribed in the Act or this Decree, shall be prescribed by the articles of incorporation or internal regulations of the Authority.
⑦ The Recommendation Committee shall prepare, retain and disclose meeting minutes recording its deliberations and resolutions: Provided, That it may refrain from disclosure where any of the subparagraphs of Article 9 (1) of the Official Information Disclosure Act applies.

Article 7 (Applications for approval of implementation plans)
① Where an Authority intends to establish and obtain approval of an implementation plan (including amendments) for works relating to new installation, reconstruction, maintenance, repair, dredging, etc. of port facilities under Article 8 (1) 1 and 4 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "port facility works") pursuant to Article 22 (1) of the Act, it shall submit an application for approval to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries accompanied by the following documents: Provided, That in the case of a new port construction project under Article 2, subparagraph 2 of the New Harbor Construction Promotion Act, the application shall instead be accompanied by the documents under the subparagraphs of Article 10 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of that Act. <Amended on 2012.7.20, 2013.3.23>
1. A location map at a scale of at least 1:5,000 and cadastral survey results indicating the area subject to permission;
2. Detailed design documents for the works (including security and safety facilities and equipment);
3. Financing plans, including annual investment and funding plans;
4. Environmental impact assessment reports under Article 27 of the Environmental Impact Assessment Act and consultation results under Article 29 of that Act (limited to projects falling within the scope of Article 31 (2) and attached Table 3 of its Enforcement Decree), and traffic impact analyses and improvement measures under Article 15 of the Urban Traffic Improvement Promotion Act and matters requiring improvement under Article 16 (4) of that Act (limited to projects under Article 13-2 (3) and attached Table 1 of its Enforcement Decree);
5. Energy use plans under Article 10 (1) of the Energy Use Rationalization Act (limited to projects falling within the scope of Article 20 (4) and attached Table 1 of its Enforcement Decree);
6. Other documents deemed necessary by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries in connection with the works.
② Where deemed necessary, the Minister may approve implementation plans for separate stages of port facility works. <Amended on 2013.3.23>
③ "Facility works prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as paving of storage yards and construction of warehouses" in the proviso to Article 22 (1) of the Act means the following port facility works: <Amended on 2013.3.23, 2020.7.28, 2021.1.5>
1. Maintenance and repair of mooring facilities such as quay walls and small-vessel wharves under Article 2, subparagraph 5 (a) of the Harbor Act;
2. Paving of storage yards under Article 18 (4) 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Harbor Act;
3. Installation (including extension, reconstruction, maintenance and repair) of warehouses, sheds and auction markets under Article 18 (4) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Harbor Act;
4. Installation of weighing facilities and of cargo-handling equipment among cargo-handling facilities under Article 18 (4) 3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Harbor Act (excluding such equipment installed at mooring facilities among basic facilities under Article 2, subparagraph 5 (a) of the Harbor Act), cargo transfer facilities (excluding offshore oil or gas pipeline facilities), and piping facilities;
5. Dredging of water facilities, including fairways and anchorage areas, under Article 18 (4) 5 of the Enforcement Decree of the Harbor Act;
6. Other port facility works determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.
④ "Period prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the latter part of Article 23 (4) of the Act means 60 days from the date the head of the competent administrative agency receives notice of a request for consultation. <Amended on 2023.12.12>

Article 8 (Matters to be publicly notified concerning implementation plans and posting)
① The matters to be publicly notified in the Official Gazette under Article 22 (3) of the Act shall be:
1. Name and address of the implementer of port facility works;
2. Name of the port and type of port facility works;
3. Purpose of the works;
4. Location, scale, period and method of implementation;
5. Other details necessary for implementation of the works.
② The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall post the details publicly notified under paragraph ① in a conspicuous place accessible to interested persons within the proposed works area. <Amended on 2013.3.23>

Article 9 (Completion inspections)
Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries conducts a completion inspection of port facility works under Article 24 (2) of the Act and the works fall under any of the following, the Minister may substitute verification of the corresponding documents for a completion inspection: <Amended on 2010.12.13, 2012.8.28, 2013.3.23, 2014.5.22, 2015.6.1, 2020.1.7, 2021.2.9, 2021.9.14>
1. Works subject to construction project management performed on behalf of the supervisory authority under Article 55 (1) 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Construction Technology Promotion Act: Construction project management records and completion inspection records prepared by a construction engineering business operator;
2. Dredging works: Copies of marine information under Article 21 of the Act on Marine Surveys and the Use of Marine Information;
3. Building construction works: Certificates of approval for use under Article 22 of the Building Act.

Article 10 Deleted. <2007.4.4>

Article 11 Deleted. <2010.4.29>

Article 12 (Use or lease of port facilities)
① Methods of using or leasing port facilities under Article 29 of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on 2013.3.23>
1. Obtaining permission for use from the Authority;
2. Concluding a usage or lease agreement with the Authority;
3. Reporting to the Authority as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries (limited to use of port facilities for the arrival or departure of passengers or cargo).
② A person seeking to use or lease port facilities under paragraph ① shall submit an application to the Authority specifying the following as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries: <Amended on 2013.3.23>
1. Address and name of the applicant;
2. Purpose of use or lease;
3. Location, name and scale of the port facilities to be used or leased;
4. Period of use or lease.

Article 13 (Types of usage fees and rents)
① Types of usage fees and rents that may be collected by an Authority (including a corporation entrusted with the management and operation of passenger terminals, etc. under Article 42 (1) of the Act; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article and Articles 14, 15 and 15-2) under Article 30 (1) of the Act shall be: <Amended on 2012.8.28>
1. Usage fees:
(a) Vessel charges;
(b) Cargo charges;
(c) Passenger terminal usage fees;
(d) Exclusive-use charges;
(e) Deleted. <2012.8.28>
2. Rents:
(a) Wharf facility rents;
(b) Cargo-handling equipment rents;
(c) Warehouse facility rents;
(d) Container handling yard rents;
(e) Port hinterland complex rents;
(f) Rents for land, buildings, etc. other than port facilities.
② Details and classification of usage fees and rents under paragraph ① 1 and 2 shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. <Newly Inserted on 2012.8.28; Amended on 2013.3.23>
③ Where a person operating a marine passenger transport business, marine cargo transport business or shipping agency business under the Marine Transportation Act at port facilities outside the port areas over which the Authority exercises jurisdiction under Article 4 (4) of the Act simultaneously transports multiple passengers or cargo consignments, reports to the Authority under Article 12 (1) 3 of this Decree and pays the port facility usage fees in a lump sum on behalf of others, the Authority may pay that operator expenses incurred in making the payment as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on 2012.8.28, 2013.3.23, 2017.6.20>

Article 14 (Exemptions from usage fees)
① Usage fees may be reduced or exempted in whole or part under Article 30 (2) of the Act where: <Amended on 2012.8.28, 2013.3.23, 2020.7.28>
1. The State or a local government uses the facilities for administrative purposes;
2. Foreign warships, government vessels, survey vessels, training ships, etc. use the facilities under the principle of reciprocity with foreign countries;
3. The facilities are used to repair vessels;
4. A nonprofit corporation relating to shipping and ports uses the facilities;
5. The facilities are used to promote the welfare of seafarers and port workers;
6. A fisheries cooperative established under the Fisheries Cooperatives Act uses the facilities to conduct fishers' businesses;
7. The facilities are used to perform any of the following port facility works:
(a) Port facility works ordered by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or an Authority;
(b) Port facility works under the main sentence of Article 9 (2) of the Harbor Act (limited to facilities that vest in the State);
8. An implementer of a port development project approved under Article 10 (2) of the Harbor Act within an Authority's jurisdiction uses port facilities within its jurisdiction to recover total project costs incurred in constructing port facilities that vested in the State under Article 15 (1) of the same Act;
9. The Authority deems use necessary for development, management or operation of a port.
② Where usage fees are exempted under paragraph ①, the Authority shall determine the scope, period and procedures of exemption following consultation with the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on 2012.8.28, 2013.3.23>

Article 15 (Changes or adjustments to usage fees)
① Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems that reported usage fee rates hinder smooth port logistics, the Minister may order the Authority to change or adjust the rates under Article 30 (4) of the Act. <Amended on 2013.3.23>
② When issuing a disposition under paragraph ①, the Minister shall give the president of the Authority an opportunity to submit opinions orally or by electronic document, etc. and may hear the opinions of interested parties where deemed necessary. <Amended on 2013.3.23>

Article 15-2 (Entrustment of collection)
① Where an Authority entrusts collection of usage fees or rents to a Special Self-Governing Province Governor or the head of a Si, Gun or Gu (meaning an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) under Article 30-2 of the Act, it shall send a written entrustment specifying the name and address of each person subject to collection, the amount and grounds for collection, the payment period and other necessary matters. <Amended on 2017.6.20>
② "Amount prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 30-2 (2) of the Act means an amount equivalent to ten percent of the amount collected by the Special Self-Governing Province Governor or head of a Si, Gun or Gu. <Amended on 2017.6.20>

Article 16 (Transfer of profit reserves, etc. to capital)
① Where the Authority intends to transfer profit reserves or business expansion reserves to capital under Article 32 (3) of the Act, it shall obtain approval from the Minister of Finance and Economy following a resolution by the Committee. <Amended on 2025.12.30>
② Where the Authority transfers profit reserves or business expansion reserves to capital under paragraph ①, it shall report that fact to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries. <Amended on 2013.3.23>

Article 17 (Issuance of bonds)
① Where the Authority intends to issue bonds under Article 34 (1) of the Act, it shall establish a bond issuance plan for the relevant year containing the following matters and obtain a resolution by the Committee: <Amended on 2017.6.20>
1. Purpose of issuing bonds;
2. Method of issuance;
3. Matters under Article 20 (2) 2 through 7;
4. Use of bond proceeds.
② The Authority shall obtain approval from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries under the former part of Article 34 (2) of the Act for the bond issuance plan resolved by the Committee under paragraph ① within two months from the date on which the Authority's budget bill is finalized under the main sentence of Article 40 (4) of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions. <Newly Inserted on 2017.6.20>
③ Where the Authority intends to amend a bond issuance plan approved under the latter part of Article 34 (2) of the Act, it shall obtain a resolution by the Committee and approval from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries at least ten days before the date of issue. <Newly Inserted on 2017.6.20>

Article 18 (Form of bonds)
Bonds shall be bearer bonds: Provided, That registered bonds may be issued at the request of subscribers or holders.

Article 19 (Methods for issuing bonds)
Bonds issued by an Authority under Article 34 (1) of the Act shall be issued by public subscription, full underwriting or sale.

Article 20 (Subscription for bonds)
① A person seeking to subscribe for bonds shall specify the number of bonds, subscription price and subscriber's address on two bond subscription forms, sign and seal them, and submit them: Provided, That where bonds are issued at a specified minimum price, the bidding price shall be stated.
② Bond subscription forms under paragraph ① shall be prepared by the president of the Authority and shall include:
1. Name of the Authority;
2. Total amount of bonds to be issued;
3. Face value by bond denomination;
4. Interest rate;
5. Method and deadline for redemption;
6. Method and deadline for payment of interest;
7. Issue price or minimum issue price;
8. Total amount of outstanding bonds, if any;
9. Name and address of the company entrusted with soliciting subscriptions, if any.

Article 21 (Full underwriting)
Article 20 shall not apply where a specified underwriter subscribes for the entire amount of bonds under an agreement with the Authority. Article 20 shall also not apply to any portion of bonds subscribed for by a company entrusted with soliciting bond subscriptions.

Article 22 (Total amount of bonds issued)
Where an Authority issues bonds under Article 34 (1) of the Act, it may state on the bond subscription form that bonds will be issued even where the amount actually subscribed for falls short of the total amount specified on the form. In such cases, the amount actually subscribed for shall be the total amount of bonds issued.

Article 23 (Issue of bonds by sale)
Where an Authority intends to issue bonds by sale, it shall publicly announce the sale period and matters under Article 20 (2) 1 through 7. In such cases, it shall not prepare bond subscription forms.

Article 24 (Payment of subscription prices of bonds)
① Upon completion of subscription for bonds, an Authority shall require subscribers to pay the full prices of the bonds subscribed for without delay.
② A company entrusted with soliciting bond subscriptions may carry out the act under paragraph ① in its own name on behalf of the Authority. In such cases, the entrusted company may prepare bond subscription forms.
③ Where bonds are issued through public subscription, the Authority shall not issue the bonds until the full amount corresponding to the total issue amount has been paid.

Article 25 (Matters to be stated on bonds)
The following shall be stated on bonds and the president of the Authority shall sign and seal them: Provided, That where bonds are issued by sale, the matters under Article 20 (2) 2 shall not be stated:
1. Matters under Article 20 (2) 1 through 6;
2. Bond number;
3. Date of bond issue.

Article 26 (Bond register)
① The Authority shall maintain at its principal office a bond register recording:
1. Number and serial numbers of bonds by denomination;
2. Date of bond issue;
3. Matters under Article 20 (2) 2 through 6 and 9.
② In the case of registered bonds, the Authority shall record:
1. Matters under the subparagraphs of paragraph ①;
2. Names and addresses of bondholders;
3. Dates of acquisition of the bonds.
③ Bondholders or persons possessing bonds may demand to inspect the bond register at any time during the Authority's business hours.

Article 27 (Missing interest coupons)
① Where bearer bonds with interest coupons are redeemed and any coupon is missing, the amount corresponding to the missing coupon shall be deducted from the amount of redemption.
② The holder of a coupon under paragraph ① may demand payment of the amount deducted upon presentation of the coupon.

Article 28 (Notices and demands to bondholders, etc.)
① Before bonds are issued, notices or demands to subscribers or persons entitled to receive the bonds shall be sent to the addresses stated on their bond subscription forms: Provided, That where another address has been notified to the Authority, that address shall be used.
② Notices or demands to holders of registered bonds shall be sent to the addresses stated in the bond register: Provided, That where another address has been notified to the Authority, that address shall be used.
③ Notices or demands to holders of bearer bonds shall be given by public announcement: Provided, That where the holder's address is known, notices or demands may be sent to that address.

Article 28-2 (Subsidization of expenses, etc.)
"Projects prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 36 of the Act means projects concerning the management and operation of outer harbor facilities or port transportation facilities among basic facilities under Article 2, subparagraph 5 (a) of the Harbor Act.

Article 29 (Guidance and supervision)
"Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, including port safety" in Article 37 of the Act means any of the following: <Amended on 2013.3.23, 2017.6.20, 2021.1.5>
1. Security, safety and protection of port facilities;
2. Convenience and safety of users of port facilities;
3. Scale of berths at passenger wharves (meaning locations for mooring vessels) to ensure smooth passenger transportation;
3-2. Operation of wharves for the smooth handling of cargo;
4. Informatization of port logistics, including standardization of logistics information and interconnection of systems;
5. Acquisition and disposal of basic property, investment, contributions, and borrowing of funds deemed closely related to the Authority's financial soundness;
6. Appropriateness of port development plans that significantly affect the public nature of port management;
7. Other matters prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 29-2 (Delegation of authority)
The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall delegate the following powers under Article 41 of the Act to the head of the regional office of oceans and fisheries having jurisdiction over the area of the Authority: <Amended on 2013.3.23, 2017.6.20, 2023.12.12>
1. Approval and public notification in the Official Gazette of implementation plans under Article 22 of the Act (including amendments);
1-2. Consultation with heads of competent administrative agencies under the former part of Article 23 (4) of the Act;
2. Confirmation of completion, issuance of certificates of completion confirmation, public notification in the Official Gazette and acceptance of reports of use before completion under Article 24 of the Act;
3. Loans of State property without charge and permission to use and profit from such property under Article 27 (1) of the Act;
4. Approval of subleases of State property under Article 28 (2) of the Act;
4-2. Reports and reports of changes concerning the types and rates of security charges under Article 42 (3) of the Act;
4-3. Orders to change or adjust the rates of security charges under Article 42 (4) of the Act;
5. Imposition and collection of administrative fines under Article 45 (2) of the Act;
6. Guidance and supervision of the Authority under Article 29, subparagraphs 1 through 4 of this Decree.

Article 29-3 (Corporations entrusted with business)
"Corporations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 42 (1) of the Act means corporations falling under any of the following: <Amended on 2020.7.28>
1. Port management corporations designated under Article 23 (1) of the Harbor Act;
2. Korea Shipping Association established under the Korea Shipping Association Act.

Article 29-4 Deleted. <2020.3.3>

Article 30 (Criteria for imposition of administrative fines)
① An administrative fine of one million won shall be imposed on a person who uses the name "Port Authority" in violation of Article 9 of the Act pursuant to Article 45 (1) of the Act.
② The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may reduce the fine under paragraph ① by up to one half after considering the motive, details and severity of the violation. <Amended on 2013.3.23>

ADDENDA <Presidential Decree No. 18147, 2003.11.29>

